1. Database Overview
Database Name

healthcare_ai_db

Purpose

The database is designed to support the AI Healthcare Analytics & Clinical Decision Support System. It manages patient records, clinical data, hospital operations, AI-generated predictions, billing, laboratory reports, and administrative activities while maintaining data integrity, scalability, and security.

Database Type
Relational Database Management System (RDBMS)
Database Engine
MySQL 8.0.46
Primary Key Strategy
UUID (CHAR(36)) for all master and transaction tables.
Audit Columns

Every table contains:

Created_At
Updated_At

2. Patients Table
Category

Master Table

Purpose
Stores complete patient demographic information.
Maintains patient contact and identification details.
Records lifestyle and health-related information.
Acts as the primary entity for appointments, diagnoses, admissions, billing, AI predictions, and reports.
Primary Key
Patient_ID
Relationships
Patients → Appointments (1:N)
Patients → Admissions (1:N)
Patients → Diagnoses (1:N)
Patients → Lab Results (1:N)
Patients → Billing (1:N)
Patients → Disease Risk Predictions (1:N)

3. Departments Table
Category

Master Table

Purpose
Stores hospital departments.
Supports doctor allocation.
Enables department-wise analytics.
Maintains operational information.
Primary Key
Department_ID
Relationships
Departments → Doctors (1:N)
Departments → Appointments (1:N)

4. Doctors Table
Category

Master Table

Purpose
Stores doctor profiles.
Maintains qualifications and specialization.
Supports appointments, diagnoses, and prescriptions.
Links each doctor to a department.
Primary Key
Doctor_ID
Foreign Key
Department_ID → Departments.Department_ID
Relationships
Doctors → Appointments (1:N)
Doctors → Diagnoses (1:N)
Doctors → Prescriptions (1:N)

5. Diseases Table
Category

Master Table

Purpose
Stores diseases supported by the system.
Maintains ICD-10 codes.
Stores disease categories and clinical information.
Provides reference data for diagnoses and AI prediction models.
Primary Key
Disease_ID
Relationships
Diseases → Diagnoses (1:N)
Diseases → AI Disease Prediction (1:N)

6. Medications Table
Category

Master Table

Purpose
Stores medication master data.
Supports prescriptions.
Maintains dosage and administration details.
Stores contraindications and side effects.
Primary Key
Medication_ID
Relationships
Medications → Prescription Items (1:N)

7. Lab Tests Table
Category

Master Table

Purpose
Stores all laboratory tests offered by the hospital.
Maintains standard test details and reference ranges.
Supports laboratory report generation.
Used for diagnosis and AI-based clinical analysis.
Standardizes laboratory investigations across departments.
Primary Key
Lab_Test_ID
Relationships
Lab Tests → Patient Lab Results (1:N)
Business Rules
Every test must have a unique name.
Every test code must be unique.
A laboratory test can appear in multiple patient reports.
Only active tests can be assigned to patients.

8. Insurance Providers Table
Category

Master Table

Purpose
Stores insurance company information.
Maintains provider contact details.
Supports billing and claim processing.
Enables insurance-based financial analytics.
Primary Key
Insurance_ID
Relationships
Insurance Providers → Patient Insurance (1:N) (to be created later)
Insurance Providers → Billing (1:N)
Business Rules
Every insurance provider must have a unique name.
Provider codes must be unique.
Only active providers can be linked to patient insurance records.

9. Appointments Table
Category

Transaction Table

Purpose
Stores all patient appointments.
Connects patients, doctors, and departments.
Tracks appointment status and consultation mode.
Supports OPD scheduling and reporting.
Serves as the entry point for diagnoses and prescriptions.
Primary Key
Appointment_ID
Foreign Keys
Patient_ID → Patients.Patient_ID
Doctor_ID → Doctors.Doctor_ID
Department_ID → Departments.Department_ID
Relationships
Patients → Appointments (1:N)
Doctors → Appointments (1:N)
Departments → Appointments (1:N)
Business Rules
Every appointment must be linked to one patient.
Every appointment must be assigned to one doctor.
Every appointment belongs to one department.
Appointment date and time are mandatory.
Status must be one of: Scheduled, Completed, Cancelled, or No Show.

10. Admissions Table
Category

Transaction Table

Purpose
Stores all inpatient admission records.
Tracks patient hospitalization.
Maintains ward, room, and bed allocation.
Supports discharge management.
Used for occupancy analytics and patient flow analysis.
Primary Key
Admission_ID
Foreign Keys
Patient_ID → Patients
Department_ID → Departments
Admitting_Doctor_ID → Doctors
Relationships
Patients → Admissions (1:N)
Departments → Admissions (1:N)
Doctors → Admissions (1:N)
Business Rules
Every admission must belong to one patient.
Every admission must be assigned to one department.
Every admission must have one admitting doctor.
Admission date is mandatory.
A patient may have multiple admissions over time.

11. Diagnoses Table
Category

Transaction Table

Purpose
Stores diagnoses made by doctors.
Links patients with diseases.
Captures symptoms, severity, and clinical notes.
Forms the primary dataset for AI disease prediction and clinical decision support.
Primary Key
Diagnosis_ID
Foreign Keys
Patient_ID → Patients
Doctor_ID → Doctors
Disease_ID → Diseases
Appointment_ID → Appointments
Admission_ID → Admissions
Relationships
Patients → Diagnoses (1:N)
Doctors → Diagnoses (1:N)
Diseases → Diagnoses (1:N)
Business Rules
Every diagnosis must reference one patient.
Every diagnosis must reference one disease.
A diagnosis can originate from an appointment or an admission.
Diagnosis status tracks confirmed and suspected conditions.

12. Prescriptions Table
Category

Transaction Table

Purpose
Stores prescriptions issued by doctors.
Links treatment plans to diagnoses.
Records follow-up advice and prescription status.
Serves as the parent table for prescription medicines.
Primary Key
Prescription_ID
Foreign Keys
Diagnosis_ID → Diagnoses
Patient_ID → Patients
Doctor_ID → Doctors
Relationships
Diagnoses → Prescriptions (1:N)
Patients → Prescriptions (1:N)
Doctors → Prescriptions (1:N)
Business Rules
Every prescription must belong to a diagnosis.
One diagnosis may generate multiple prescriptions.
A prescription can include multiple medicines (stored in the next table).

